  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and both now exist in Kibana. The rule is turned on and its first run came back with status \"ok\".\n\n**1. What the data holds.** Each record in `kibana_sample_data_logs` is one web request. The main fields are:\n- `timestamp`, the request time\n- `response`, the HTTP status code, which is stored as text rather than a number\n- `bytes`, the response size\n- `clientip`, the visitor's address\n- `url` and `request`, the page asked for\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, the source and destination country\n- `machine.os` and `agent`, the visitor's system and browser\n- `extension`, `referer` and `tags`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-d2792cef Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-d2792cef-web-logs-overview-ad338dce9bbc\n- Three single numbers: total requests, unique visitors (counted by distinct client IP) and total bytes served.\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of the share of each response code.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and bytes for each.\n- A bar chart of requests by source country.\n\nI haven't opened the dashboard to check that every panel shows data. Kibana only confirmed that it was created.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-d2792cef 5xx spike\"** (id `a70af07e-98e3-4206-82f0-965d5fa4a0a4`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Schedule:** it runs every minute.\n- **What it counts:** requests whose response code is from 500 to 599. Because the code is stored as text, the rule compares it as text, which works correctly for three-digit codes.\n- **When it fires:** when more than 10 of those requests have a `timestamp` within the last 5 minutes.\n- **No notification set up:** the rule has no connector attached, so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana and doesn't email or message anyone.",
